ios: landing menu

A touch acts on what was under the finger when it landed. The minimenu (what a tap does, what a long-press opens)
was rebuilt every frame from the finger's position, so a long-press opened what was there 0.4 s later: a walking
NPC had often left the spot, and while the player walks the camera follows it and the ground slides under a held
finger. Now the first frame drawn after pointerdown builds the minimenu and later frames keep it (holdsLandingMenu)
while the finger can still tap or long-press (held within the slop, no camera turn, drag or scroll), and after it
lifts until its tap's click has been handled. Its NPC and player options name the NPC or player, so they follow one
that walked off. That frame also finds the ground tile under the finger (renderAll's click pick, cancelled before it
walks: World.cancelMousePicking), and its "Walk here" walks there (menuGroundX/Z, walkToMenuGround), from a tap or
from the long-press menu however long it stays open. The tooltip shows the kept minimenu. A change of the open
windows, tab, use or target mode or map drops it, and the tap acts on what is there then. Touch screens only.

